<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Said bin Taimur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-said-dynasty">Al Bu Said dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Said bin Taimur was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> from 1932 to 1970. He kept the country isolated and frugal, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-akhdar-war">Jebel Akhdar War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of Oman with British help and sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwadar">Gwadar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1958. Even after oil exports began in 1967 development stayed slow, and his son Qaboos deposed him in 1970.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/said-bin-taimur.jpg" alt="Said bin Taimur" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Library: India Office Records and Private Papers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Said_Bin_Timur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Said bin Taimur was born in 1910 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-said-dynasty">Al Bu Said dynasty</a>. His father, Sultan Taimur bin Feisal, was strongly against his learning Western ways or English, and once ordered the books of Said and his brother Nadir burned after finding an English primer. Said nonetheless attended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayo-college">Mayo College</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1922 to 1927 and mastered English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>. His father feared that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> would expose him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, so he was sent instead to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> for a year to study Arabic literature and history. On his return he joined the government and in August 1929 became president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of Ministers</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In February 1932, at 21, Said became sultan after his father abdicated for financial reasons. He inherited an administration in debt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and from 1933 he controlled the state budget. With British help he regained control of the tribal interior, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> together, and then lived in a coastal town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a>. Britain recognised Oman's independence in 1951. In 1938 he visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> at the invitation of President Franklin Roosevelt, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he cooperated with Britain, allowing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> landing fields to be built between Salalah and Muscat.</p><p>He faced opposition from Imam Ghalib bin Ali, whose revolt in Jebel Akhdar was suppressed in 1955 with British help. Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> supported the Imam, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> disliked British involvement; both backed a renewed revolt in 1957, which was suppressed by 1959. On 8 September 1958 Said handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwadar">Gwadar</a> over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. In 1965 he made concessions to export oil to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and the same year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a> revolted, with support from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> and some nationalist Arab states. After an assassination attempt in 1966 his rule became more erratic: smoking in public, football and sunglasses were forbidden. At the end of his reign there were three schools, a literacy rate of 5% and 10 kilometres of paved roads.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His son Qaboos, back from the Royal Military Academy and a year in the British Army, was kept under house arrest at Al Hosn Palace in Salalah, and Said did not speak to him for the last 14 months before the coup. On 23 July 1970 Qaboos deposed him at the palace in Salalah with British help and his uncle's, and sent him into exile in the United Kingdom. Said spent his last two years at the Dorchester Hotel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and died on 19 October 1972. He was first buried at Brookwood Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and his remains were later taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and buried in the royal cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Said bin Taimur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sultan <b>Said bin Taimur's</b> forces, backed by Britain, occupied Nizwa in 1955, and the Jebel Akhdar War ended in 1959 when British troops stormed the mountain.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-oman">Imamate of Oman</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Said bin Taimur</b>, sultan from 1932, kept the country isolated, won the Jebel Akhdar War of 1954-1959 and sold Gwadar to Pakistan in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</a></li><li><blockquote>On 23 July 1970 Qaboos bin Said deposed his father <b>Said bin Taimur</b>, ending the isolation of the Sultanate of Muscat and Oman, and renamed the country the Sultanate of Oman.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-oman">Sultanate of Oman</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.61,58.59&amp;year=1950" aria-label="Muscat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6112 1293 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Said bin Taimur"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6562 1563h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6562 1563h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.61,58.59&amp;year=1950">What was here in 1950? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
